From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] aic_load_fw: track the firmware upload per part, not in one global flag

`int fw_loaded` was file-scope, non-static and module-wide. It is asked a
per-part question, so with more than one AIC part on a host the answer for one
part is decided by what happened to another.

The complete inventory (line numbers as in tag 5.0+git20260123.5f7be68d-8; an earlier patch of this series shifts
the last two to 1769/1787, and does not move the first two):

  write  aicwf_usb.c:42    int fw_loaded = 0;      definition, the only initialiser
  write  aicwf_usb.c:1781  fw_loaded = 1;          end of a successful probe,
                                                   immediately after aicfw_download_fw()
  read   aicwf_usb.c:1685  site 1 -- post-download id AND loaded -> refuse
  read   aicwf_usb.c:1763  site 2 -- post-download id AND NOT loaded ->
                                     rwnx_send_reboot(), push it back to download mode

That is all of them, in both modules. There is no EXPORT_SYMBOL, so
aic8800_fdrv cannot read it, and nothing resets it -- not aicwf_usb_disconnect(),
not aic_bluetooth_mod_exit(). It is a one-way latch for the module's lifetime.

Why the product id cannot carry this, which was the first thing tried
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Both read sites fire only for the post-download identities, and it is the same
set of parts on both: site 1 keys on id->idProduct {8801, 8d81, 8d41, 8d91,
8d99}, site 2 on usb_dev->chipid, and aicloadfw_chipmatch() maps 8d81 and 8d41
onto PRODUCT_ID_AIC8800D81 and 8801 onto PRODUCT_ID_AIC8801. So the id selects
which *pair* of branches applies, and the flag is the only thing choosing
between them. Deriving the flag from the id collapses the pair, and both
collapses are wrong:

  always refuse  -- a part that comes up already at its loaded id is never
                    rebooted into download mode, so our firmware never reaches it
  always reboot  -- a normal cold boot loops: 8d80 -> download -> 8d81 -> reboot
                    -> 8d80 -> download -> ...

The 8d81 descriptor is byte-identical whether we produced it 750 ms ago or a
previous boot did; there is no counter, timestamp or provenance field in it.
This is an information argument, not a preference: the state is genuinely
needed, and it is per part.

Where the state has to live
---------------------------
It must survive the download re-enumeration, because that is the gap it spans.
Measured on the board, one boot of the delivery image:

  usb 2-1.1: new high-speed USB device number 3      <- a69c:8d80
  usb 2-1.1: USB disconnect, device number 3
  usb 2-1.1: new high-speed USB device number 4      <- a69c:8d81

So usb_set_intfdata() is out (the interfaces are destroyed) and anything hung
off struct usb_device is out (a new one is allocated, new device number). What
does not change is the USB topology path -- both lines above say "2-1.1" -- and
that is what this patch keys on. dev_name(&udev->dev) is "<busnum>-<devpath>"
with devpath a char[16] (include/linux/usb.h:663), so the 32-byte slots cannot
truncate. drivers/usb/core/usb.c:686-690 warns that the busnum half is not
stable across HCD modprobe order; that does not reach this state, which only
has to live from one probe to the next inside a single download cycle.

What actually goes wrong today
------------------------------
Part A on one port at its download id, part B on another already at its loaded
id -- B left powered across a kernel reload, or a dongle carrying firmware from
another host. Correctness then depends on enumeration order:

  A first: A downloads, sets the flag. B hits site 1, is refused, is never
           rebooted, never receives our firmware. Silent dark radio.
  B first: B hits site 2, is rebooted into download mode, downloads. A downloads.
           Both work.

With this patch B's record is empty regardless of what A did, so site 2 fires
either way and the order stops mattering.

⚠ The module-reload path is NOT this bug, and an earlier note of mine implying
it was is wrong. rmmod frees the module's data segment, so modprobe starts with
fw_loaded == 0 -- which is the correct value for a part that is already at its
loaded id. The reload path takes site 2 and works; this patch leaves it exactly
as it was (a fresh module has an empty array, which is the same statement).

Risk
----
This sits on the decision that gates firmware download on every boot, so the
relevant question is whether the single-part path can change. It cannot, by
construction: with one part there is exactly one path string, so the array is
the old one-bit flag that happens to carry a name. Cold boot -> array empty ->
aicwf_fw_part_loaded() false -> site 1 cannot fire, site 2 cannot fire (8d80 is
not a post-download id) -> download -> mark. Re-enumeration as 8d81 -> the path
is found -> true -> site 1 fires. That is bit-for-bit the old state machine.
The only divergence is between two different paths, which is the broken case.

The table is a fixed four slots rather than a list because this driver serves
one soldered part plus at most a stray dongle. A full table answers "already
loaded", and that direction is deliberate: the opposite would reboot a part it
cannot record, which returns under its loaded id, is not recorded again, and
loops forever. Refusing is what the single global did for every part anyway.

Verified on the previous pin, 4.0+git20250410.b99ca8b6-5: applies clean in
sequence; aicwf_usb.o compiles
(native aarch64, 6.18.36, 0 errors, only the two pre-existing vendor
-Wmissing-prototypes). In the built object the global is gone and the
replacement is entirely file-local -- `B fw_loaded` before, `b aicwf_fw_part` /
`b aicwf_fw_part_n` / `d aicwf_fw_part_lock` / `t aicwf_fw_part_loaded` after.

Ported to 5.0+git20260123.5f7be68d-8 with all five hunks unchanged: the tag only
moves the probe sites down by four lines (a new 8800D40X2 branch in
aicloadfw_chipmatch()) and still has exactly these four fw_loaded sites. Applies
clean in sequence there; not yet compiled.

NOT hardware-proven: this has never booted, and the two-part case it fixes
needs a second AIC part, which we do not have. Site 2 (rwnx_send_reboot) has
still never executed on any board here -- unchanged by this patch, and the one
concrete observable that would settle it is a modprobe of the loader alone
against a part already at 8d81.

